<section class="wrap page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Recent work</p><h1>What we've built, and who it was for</h1><p class="lede">A few projects from the past year. Some clients we can name. Others we can't, so we describe the work instead.</p></section>
<section class="wrap page-body">
<div class="section-rule" id="inclusion-academy"><h2>Special Olympics Inclusion Academy: an AI-built interactive course</h2><p>Special Olympics already had a self-paced course on inclusion in schools. They wanted educators to practice it, not just page through it. So we designed the Interactive Experience and built it entirely with AI: the interactions, the code, first-draft translations and the testing. Their experts owned the content and approved every language.</p><p>It's live in six languages, and it records what learners actually decide and write, not just whether they finished. Plenty of people talk about AI-built learning. This one is live.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="https://www.specialolympics.org/InclusionAcademy" rel="noopener">See the Academy</a></div></div>
<div class="section-rule" id="adoption-sprint"><h2>An AI adoption sprint for a national code consulting firm</h2><p>Our client is a national fire, life safety and accessibility code consulting firm. No developers, no data team. Just code experts with real problems: proposals that varied from person to person, scattered workflows, and knowledge living in a few people's heads.</p><p>Their Director of Training &amp; Development stepped up to lead AI, and we partnered with her on a sprint. We ran activation workshops and AI jams, coached internal champions, and prototyped tools against their actual work. We closed it out with a leadership readout, a recommendations report and an interactive ROI calculator.</p></div>
<div class="section-rule" id="full-day-lab"><h2>A full-day AI lab for a talent assessment company</h2><p>A talent assessment company brought us on-site to run a full day of AI for their team. Before the day, we met one-on-one with several people on the team to find where AI could actually help their work.</p><p>The morning was about mindset: why AI feels different, and why it works better when you treat it like someone you coach. Then hands-on time with their own workflows. In the afternoon, the group built an AI-first answer to a real problem from their business, broke out to apply it to their own work, and left with assigned next steps instead of a pile of ideas.</p></div>
<div class="section-rule" id="half-day-workshop"><h2>A half-day AI workshop for a healthcare HR leadership team</h2><p>An HR leadership team at a national healthcare company stretched the second day of their offsite to make room for this. Half a day, two parts. First, how AI actually works, so nobody was guessing. Then hands-on time in Copilot, the tool they already had, working on their own problems instead of made-up ones. It felt more like a rehearsal than a lecture.</p><p>Afterward, we built the team a private site with the session transcript loaded into an AI assistant, so they could keep asking questions once we left. We met again a couple of weeks later to see what stuck.</p></div>
<div class="section-rule" id="labs"><h2>Two labs we bring into sessions</h2><ul class="cards">
<li class="card"><h3><a href="https://joshpenzell.com/lab/attention" rel="noopener">What Your Eyes Tell You</a></h3><p>Predict where your attention will go, then see a heatmap of where it went. Every choice streams out as xAPI while you watch.</p></li>
<li class="card"><h3><a href="https://joshpenzell.com/lab/are-you-sure" rel="noopener">Are You Sure?</a></h3><p>A room studies a word list, writes down what they remember and rates how sure they are. Then the reveal. We use it to open sessions on checking AI work.</p></li>
</ul></div>
